        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:#2a3c54;line-height:1.73;margin:0 0 14px">Yes. A dryer that runs but does not heat can sometimes have a simpler and less expensive cause than a failed heating element. A tripped breaker or partial power issue, a wiring connection problem, or a smaller safety or sensing component can produce the same no-heat symptom at a lower repair cost.</p>
        <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:#060c19;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 24px;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;padding-left:16px">Not every dryer that runs without heat needs a heating element.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:#060c19;margin:0 0 16px;letter-spacing:-0.02em">Could It Cost More?</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:#2a3c54;line-height:1.73;margin:0">Yes. More complex scenarios can drive a higher repair cost. Multiple failed heating-system components, damaged internal wiring, control board problems, or significant airflow damage can each increase the scope of a dryer not-heating repair. None of those conditions were present in this case.</p>
